# SceneShift command guardrails

When shell tools fail, escalate carefully. Do not "fix" sandbox or Simulator errors by destroying project structure.

## Never without an explicit user request

- Delete or recreate `sceneshift.xcworkspace` or `SceneShift.xcodeproj` (`rm -rf`, move-aside-and-rebuild, empty `contents.xcworkspacedata`)
- `git reset --hard`, `git clean -fdx`, force push, or rewrite published history
- Wipe `SceneShift/`, `SceneShiftTests/`, or `SceneShiftUITests/`
- Change bundle id, deployment target, or shared scheme as a side effect of troubleshooting

## Prefer repair in place

- If the workspace looks broken, edit `sceneshift.xcworkspace/contents.xcworkspacedata` so it references `group:SceneShift.xcodeproj`
- If paths look wrong, move folders back to repo-root siblings of the `.xcodeproj` — do not invent a second project
- If `xcodebuild` says a workspace is invalid under the sandbox, retry with elevated permissions before concluding the file is bad

## Sandbox and Simulator escalation

- Default sandbox cannot use `CoreSimulatorService`. That is expected.
- Request `all` / unrestricted only for compile, `simctl`, or DerivedData writes — not for destructive cleanup
- Use repo-local `-derivedDataPath .derivedData` for agent builds
- Do not delete Simulator devices, runtimes, or DerivedData outside the repo to "unblock" a build

## Stop and ask the user when

- A fix seems to require deleting the workspace or project
- A command would remove more than the files touched by the current task
- Auto-review / policy blocks a destructive command — do not bypass by rewriting the same delete another way
- You are unsure whether a failure is sandbox vs real project corruption

## Safe verification pattern

1. `xcodebuild -list -workspace sceneshift.xcworkspace`
2. Build with `-derivedDataPath .derivedData` and `generic/platform=iOS Simulator` when only compile proof is needed
3. Use a named Simulator destination only with full host access and only when UI/runtime proof is required
